On Thursday, October 29, 2026 at 1:53:46 AM UTC-4 chris.h...@gmail.com wrote:
> Hi Laura,
>
> If you omit some categories, then your reference function is effectively the combined category of omitted categories.
>
> The reference category should not be rare. By default in ctmm, the reference category is taken to be the most common category. If the reference category has no data in it (or nearby available area for an RSF) then that's the same as not having a reference category at all.
>
> Categorical data are necessarily collinear if you try to use every category as an independent covariate, because they are dependent. If you are in one category then you can't be in any other category. The same thing happens if you choose a bad reference category with no data in it.
>
> For an RSF, it's not the raw amount of data but how many home-range crossings there are in that 12 months. What is the home-range crossing timescale, tau[position]?
>
> Another thing that can cause insignificant coefficients is having too many categories for the amount of data. Sometimes categories need to be meaningfully grouped together to get significant results.
>
> Since the ctmm models are currently stationary, you might consider subsetting the diurnal/nocturnal data to the day/night when the individuals are performing selection and not sitting in the same spot.
>
> Best,
> Chris
